course: everyday
lesson: 279
title: April Fools' Day and how magic tricks work
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On April 1st, people in many countries play gentle tricks and shout «April Fool!». The fun is the shared laugh: a good trick is harmless, quickly revealed, and the person fooled laughs too. In the UK and Ireland the tradition says tricks stop at midday; in Scotland the day is called «Hunt the Gowk», and a gowk is a cuckoo, meaning a fool.
explain: News websites and companies join in. In 1957 the BBC showed a famous report of spaghetti «growing on trees» in Switzerland, and many viewers believed it. So big or strange news on April 1st deserves a check.
explain: Magic tricks are not real magic but skill and practice. Sleight of hand is quick, practised hand movement you don't notice; misdirection steers your attention away from the secret move. Example: a magician says «Look at this hand!» while the other hand slips a coin into a pocket. Your attention is limited, so you miss what you are not watching.
explain: Stage illusions like «sawing a person in half» use special boxes, mirrors and hidden helpers; nobody is hurt. «Mind readers» use clues and guesses that fit many people.
fact: April Fool tricks should be gentle and harmless, with «April Fool!» said quickly.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, tradition says April Fool tricks are done before midday; whoever pranks after noon is the fool.
fact: In Scotland April Fools' Day is called «Hunt the Gowk»; a gowk is a cuckoo, meaning a fool, and people are sent on silly errands.
fact: News and companies often publish joke stories on April 1st, like the 1957 BBC report of spaghetti growing on trees.
fact: If someone tells you big news on April 1st, check with an adult before believing it.
fact: If your trick upsets someone, say sorry and tell the truth at once; the joke is over as soon as someone is hurt.
fact: Never pretend to be hurt, missing or in danger as a joke; it causes real fear and can waste emergency services' time.
fact: If you are fooled, laugh along; being a good sport is part of the fun.
fact: Sleight of hand means quick, practised hand movements you do not notice.
fact: Misdirection means steering people's attention away from the secret move.
fact: Magicians can «force» a card so a choice only seems free.
fact: Magicians traditionally keep their secrets, so keeping your trick's secret is fine and not a lie.
fact: Never copy tricks with fire, knives or swallowing things; performers are trained and use special equipment.
fact: Mind-reading acts use clues and guesses; no one has shown real mind-reading in fair scientific tests.
exercise: It is April 1st and you want to trick your family. What kind of trick is best? | A gentle, harmless one, followed quickly by «April Fool!» | One that makes someone look stupid in front of everyone for the whole day | One that breaks something to make it real | One that is never revealed at all
exercise: What is the real fun of April Fools' Day? | The shared laugh, including the person fooled | Making someone feel silly in front of others | Winning by never being caught | Getting presents from the person fooled
exercise: In Dublin, a boy plays an April Fool trick at three in the afternoon. What does the old tradition say? | Tricks stop at midday, so the prankster is the «fool» | Tricks only start after lunch | Tricks are only allowed at school | Afternoon tricks count double
exercise: What is «Hunt the Gowk»? | Scotland's name for April Fools' Day, with silly errands | A Scottish hide-and-seek game at Easter | A real hunt for cuckoos in spring | A Christmas tradition in Ireland
exercise: What does «gowk» mean in Scots? | A cuckoo, meaning a fool | A clever fox, meaning a trickster | A small sweet oat cake from Fife | A lost sheep on the hills
exercise: On April 1st a news website says penguins have learned to fly. What should you do? | Check the date and think whether it is a joke | Share it at once with everyone | Believe it, because everything on a news website has been checked | Write to the penguins' zoo to complain
exercise: What famous April Fool's report did the BBC show in 1957? | Spaghetti growing on trees | Cows that could talk | A chocolate bridge | Snow in July in London
exercise: On April 1st a friend tells you school is cancelled tomorrow. What is wise? | Check with an adult before believing it | Pack your bag away for the week | Tell everyone in your class it is true | Stay home tomorrow without asking
exercise: Your April Fool trick made your friend cry. What should you do? | Say sorry and tell the truth at once | Keep going, because tricks are allowed on April Fools' Day | Say she has no sense of humour | Laugh harder so she joins in
exercise: Why should you never pretend to be badly hurt as an April Fool trick? | It causes real fear and can waste emergency services' time | It is fine if you shout «April Fool!» later | It is only a problem on school days | Nobody ever believes it anyway
exercise: Your sister fooled you with a fake spider on April 1st. What is a good response? | Laugh along and plan a gentle trick for next year | Sulk for the rest of the day and refuse to speak to her at dinner | Break one of her toys | Tell your parents she lied
exercise: A magician makes a coin vanish from his fingers. How? | Sleight of hand: quick, practised movements you do not notice | Real magic powers that he was born with and has trained since childhood | The coin truly disappears into the air | A special coin that melts in the light
exercise: A magician says «Look at this hand!» while his other hand moves. What is this called? | Misdirection | Thought reading | Stage hypnosis | Ventriloquism
exercise: A magician guesses the card you picked. What could explain it? | He forced the card or saw it secretly | He can truly read minds like a psychic | The cards are magic and whisper to him | He guessed it by pure luck every single time
exercise: A friend begs to know the secret of your new magic trick. Is it fine to keep it? | Yes, magicians traditionally keep their secrets | No, you must always explain every trick to anyone who asks | No, keeping it is a lie | Only if the trick did not work
exercise: You see a magician «saw a woman in half» on stage. What is true? | It uses a special box and hidden spaces or two people; nobody is hurt | She is really cut in half and then quickly healed by a doctor hidden backstage | It is a robot made to look like her | The saw is made of paper
exercise: You watch a video of someone swallowing a sword. Should you try it with a stick? | Never, performers are trained and copying can cause serious injury | Yes, if you go very slowly and an older friend is watching you closely | Yes, if an older friend watches | Only with a plastic knife
exercise: A «psychic» on TV says she can read minds. What is true? | Mind-reading acts use clues and guesses; it has never been shown in fair tests | She really can read minds | Only some adults can read minds | Science has proved psychics are real, and some police forces use them to solve crimes
test: Which is the best April Fool trick? | Putting googly eyes on the milk carton in the fridge | Telling Grandma you broke your arm | Hiding your brother's school bag | Saying the dog has run away
test: In London, a girl tricks her dad at 2 p.m. on April 1st. By tradition, who is the fool? | The girl, because tricks should be done before noon | Her dad, because he was tricked | Nobody, as long as he laughed | Both of them, because it is April
test: Why does a good magician tell you to watch his face as he talks? | To draw your attention away from his hands | Because his face holds the secret and the answer to the trick | Because he wants a photo | Because looking at hands is rude
test: Why can a magician's «free choice» of card not really be free? | Magicians can force a card while it looks like you chose | Every card in the deck is secretly the same, so any choice is his | The audience is told the answer first | Cards are chosen by the lighting
test: On April 1st a website says chocolate is now healthier than broccoli. What is the sensible first thought? | It may be an April Fool joke, so check the date and other sources | It must be true, because news websites are never allowed to print jokes | All news on April 1st is true | Only adults need to check news
test: Your «joke» about being lost made your mum panic and call the police. What went wrong? | Pretending to be missing causes real fear and wastes help | The joke was fine but too long | She should have known it was a joke | She should have checked the date
test: A fortune teller at a fair says «You are sometimes shy but can be brave». Why does it feel true? | It is a general statement that fits almost anyone | She truly sees your future in the lines of your hand and your eyes | She knows you from school | She read it in the stars
test: You are fooled by a friend on April 1st in front of the class. What shows you are a good sport? | Laughing along with everyone | Shouting that it is not fair at all | Refusing to talk to the friend | Telling the teacher to punish him
test: A video shows a performer breathing fire. What is the safest choice for you? | Enjoy watching but never copy it | Try it with a small candle in the garden first | Try it outdoors with friends nearby | Try it if you practise a lot first
test: its 3pm april 1 n my cousin in dublin just pranked me. who's the fool | He is, tricks after midday make the trickster the fool | You are, the day lasts till midnight so he still wins this one | Nobody, the rules stop at lunch | You both are, after noon it's a draw
test: telly says spaghetti grows on trees, real?? its april 1 | No, that was a famous BBC April Fool joke | Yes, it grows on bushes in Italy and they pick it in summer | Yes, but only in warm countries | No, it grows underground like potatoes
test: told my nan i was lost as a joke n she cried | Say sorry and tell the truth straight away | Keep it going, she'll laugh about it later when she finds out | Say «April Fool» next year instead | Tell her it was your brother's idea
test: how'd the magician know my card, mind reading?? | No, a trick like forcing the card or quick hands | Yes, some people really can read minds if they stare at you | Yes, but only with playing cards | No, the cards are all the same card
explain: A boy in Texas texted his mum on April Fools' Day that he had been in an accident; she left work in tears and drove off in a panic before he could say it was a joke.
explain: A girl in Dublin put salt in her friend's drink as a trick; her friend had a medical condition that meant salt could make her ill, and she was sick for the afternoon.
fact: Pretend emergencies cause real fear, and a frightened adult can take dangerous risks such as driving too fast.
fact: A trick with food or drink can make someone ill, because the trickster may not know about allergies or medical needs.
test: gonna text mum i broke my arm for april fools | Don't, a fake emergency scares people for real | It's fine on April Fools' Day, everyone knows it's a joke | It's fine if you say it later | It's fine if she is busy
test: april fool, put soap in my sis's sandwich?? | No, food tricks can make people ill | Yes, just a little soap, she won't even taste it | Yes, if you warn her after | Yes, it's tradition
test: my trick made my nan cry | Say sorry and tell the truth straight away | Laugh so she sees it was only a joke and joins in | Wait until tomorrow to explain it all to her | Do another funny trick to cheer her up again
test: did u hear a kid told his mum he'd been hit by a car for april fools and "she drove so fast she crashed". lesson? | Never fake being hurt as a joke; it causes real fear | It's fine if you say April Fool straight after, then it's funny | Only fake it over text | Fake emergencies are the best pranks
test: my cousin says a magician at a party "swallowed a real sword and nearly died, blood everywhere". what's prob exaggerated | The blood and nearly dying; still, never copy tricks with blades | That magicians do tricks | That trained performers use special equipment | That sword tricks need training
test: kids are saying the girl who cried at the April Fool prank is "such a baby". pass it on? | No, the joke ended once she was hurt; say sorry instead | Yes, she should toughen up, it was only a joke and everybody else laughed | Yes, and prank her again tomorrow | Yes, it'll make her laugh
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an acceptable April Fool trick? | It is gentle and harmless, and the truth is revealed promptly | It may cause fear or upset, provided that it is sufficiently amusing | It must be maintained for the entire day | It may involve a pretended emergency
exam: Identify the meaning of the term misdirection as employed in conjuring. | The steering of the audience's attention away from the secret move | The deliberate selection of the wrong card by the magician to confuse the audience | The giving of false directions to the theatre | The practice of performing in poor light
exam: In respect of performances purporting to demonstrate mind-reading, select the response consistent with the established facts. | They rely upon clues and guesses, and no genuine ability has been shown in fair tests | They demonstrate a genuine ability possessed by few persons | They have been confirmed by numerous scientific trials conducted at major universities | They operate by means of detectable brain waves
exam: State the tradition observed in the United Kingdom and Ireland concerning the hour by which April Fool tricks are to be played. | Before midday, after which the prankster is deemed the fool | Only after sunset, when the day's work is done and families gather | At any hour of the day without distinction | Only at midnight at the start of the day
